Bacterial Vaginosis
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Thin grayish-white discharge, Fishy malodor, Presence of clue cells

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-004
Reason for Visit: Sudden escalation of profuse, malodorous vaginal discharge causing severe social distress and acute concern for a sexually transmitted pelvic infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute onset of profuse, strong-smelling discharge with mild lower abdominal discomfort and severe anxiety regarding a potential acute pelvic infection. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to rule out Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID) and initiate appropriate antimicrobial therapy to prevent potential ascending infection and alleviate profound psychological distress before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I suddenly have this horrible smelling discharge that is soaking my underwear, and I am terrified I have caught a serious infection.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a three-day history of a thin, homogenous, grayish-white vaginal discharge smoothly coating the vaginal walls. The symptom peaked this evening with a distinctive, strong fishy odor that became acutely noticeable and distressing after intercourse. She denies any significant vulvovaginal erythema or severe itching, though she notes mild vulvar irritation. She denies dysuria, fever, or deep dyspareunia. She admits to using an intra-vaginal douche yesterday in an attempt to clear the odor, which seemingly worsened the volume of the discharge.
Functional Status: Independent, but highly anxious and socially withdrawn tonight.
Social/Safety Concerns: No physical safety concerns; high level of acute health anxiety.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 118/76 mmHg | HR: 84 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 14 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Patient appears healthy but visibly anxious and embarrassed.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is soft, non-tender, with no suprapubic guarding. Speculum examination reveals a thin, homogenous, grayish-white discharge adherent to the vaginal walls. There is an absence of significant vulvovaginal erythema, edema, or gross inflammation. Cervix appears normal with no mucopurulent discharge. Cervical motion tenderness is absent. Vaginal pH testing indicates a pH definitively greater than 4.5. A positive whiff test is noted upon the addition of 10% potassium hydroxide to the sample.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment, no hazards noted.
Medication Reconciliation: No current prescription medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Bacterial Vaginosis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Trichomoniasis - Unlikely due to the absence of frothy, yellow-green discharge and lack of significant mucosal erythema (strawberry cervix).

Vulvovaginal Candidiasis - Unlikely given the thin, grayish discharge rather than a thick, curdy discharge, and the lack of severe pruritus.

Pelvic Inflammatory Disease - Ruled out due to the absence of fever, abdominal pain, and lack of cervical motion tenderness on examination.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe anxiety and profuse, malodorous vaginal discharge prompting concerns for an acute ascending pelvic infection. The objective clinical examination effectively ruled out Pelvic Inflammatory Disease through the absence of fever and cervical motion tenderness, whilst confirming the classic signs of Bacterial Vaginosis (elevated pH, positive whiff test, characteristic discharge). Urgent after-hours assessment was necessary to safely exclude a severe STI requiring immediate hospital-level intervention and to provide diagnostic reassurance and targeted antibiotic therapy, as the patient's acute distress and fear of a worsening infection could not be managed via delayed in-hours care.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral metronidazole 400mg twice daily for 7 days. Advised on strict avoidance of alcohol during treatment.
Positive Behavioral Change: To restore normal vaginal flora, strictly avoid using intra-vaginal douches or scented soaps; noticing the sustained absence of the fishy odor will reinforce the benefit of leaving the vaginal environment undisturbed. To ensure the infection clears, complete the entire course of antibiotics even if symptoms resolve early; understanding that this prevents immediate recurrence will drive medication compliance.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Routine GP review only required if symptoms persist after completing the antibiotic course.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Attend the Emergency Department or local urgent care if you develop a fever higher than 38.0 C, begin experiencing severe lower abdominal pain, or develop unexplained nausea and vomiting.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is highly probable based on clinical criteria; lab cultures are pending if swabs were taken.
Clinical Course: The discharge and odor should significantly improve within 48 to 72 hours of starting the antibiotics.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you develop deep pelvic pain, fevers, or if the medication causes severe vomiting.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Bacterial vaginosis is caused by an overgrowth of normal bacteria, not necessarily a sexually transmitted infection. The prescribed antibiotic specifically targets these overgrown bacteria to restore balance. Douching actually washes away the good protective bacteria, making the problem worse.
Actionable Steps: Take the antibiotics exactly as prescribed, completely avoid alcohol to prevent severe nausea, and stop all douching practices.

